
Each level is a smaller open world that also alows for a more narrow objective to be discovered and completed. Harm to game is minimal in my opinion as it still feels very fleshed out and connected with the best part being, it could acually run for the time.

It is a Puzzle and Stealth game at its core. Shooting and direct combat is highly disinsentivised with health and aiming being paper thin. Stealth in missions to avoid combat is rewared and creativity is further rewared.

Skills are required to do a task with any effectiveness. Shooting your gun or stunning an enemy with no skills is acual trash, while with some skills and mastery, you can mass one shot enemies and knock out enemys with one tap of a batton.
Your skills can only be increased with your currentcy which causes a dilema: Spend the credits on equipemnt and information in the mission or save it for skills in later missions. Normally this dilema never happens in other games because you can upgrade now or wait post mission when you get more skill points/money etc.
But the fact you might have to give up some skill buffs for more ammo, info etc durring that current mission, makes you think.

The inventory limt in not just space of slots but also the size of your equipment (How many slots one tool can take up, in a grid of your main inventory compared to the "Tool belt" inventory for stuff to quickly grab) 
forces you to plan what tools to bring and get, and what to leave and neglect. The game wouldn't be as intresting if you could bring every tool everywhere because, no need to problem solve just pull out a thousand wepeons and blow the whole place up. The challenge comes from having to dicide.

You can choose to directly disobey orders from the UNATCO which can lead to Paul Denton's death. This cause a far lonlyer game and you miss out on dialoge and nice moments between you and him.

The devs imagined a world plagued by misinformation that was weponized on the people, the rise of powerful AIs and a litteral plague coming out of China. The writers got the idea of weponized information being used in the coming century with everything (9/11,Iraq,elections,covid,etc).
The plague was less so, covid wasn't started by a secret organization to control the world by killing masses. The AIs are pretty normal and expected predictions. The main plot is also wrong but its messages of how information controll is seen as very important to corrupt regimes is very pressing.

They may have been forced to change some depictions of terrorism and having a sceret cabol just running the world, but the over arching story could still very well stay intact with some cleaning here and there for modern audiences. (The racial Sterotypes might need to go).

The graphics are very grainy and low. They try high contrasts but it just comes off as sand. The low poly doesn't look particulary bad though and the textures don't make your brain hurt. Modern games would either go more stylized like TF2 or be more realistic such as moder call of duty games.

The sound effects are more about information than immurson. The game had to, they couldn't have very high definition sounds strune all over the place with thousands of different sounds for the files and making sure they didn't break. 
The game gives you just the key information and some ambient music so you don't get too bored. Modern games give full symphony of high qaulity music, realistic sound scapes and great variety that pulls you in but can be confusing as to what it is telling you.

The New Dark Age was my ending. I destroyed both the remnants of the illuminati and AIs in the final blast. All you have to do is follow Tracer Tong's plans and cause a reactor melt down.

As long as you don't screw with Helios mid mission, you can merge with Helios and get the Helios Ending. Join the Illuminati requires you to just go and talk to Morgan Everret. All endings are pretty acheiveable no matter what.
